  "text": "Question three. How do row number, rank, and dense rank differ? Look where the scores tie. Three people have eighty-eight, and four have eighty-five. Row number gives every row its own number, so a tie is broken arbitrarily. Rank lets ties share a number, then skips: eight, eight, eight, then eleven. Dense rank shares without the gap: eight, then nine. Question four. How do you return the top N rows per group? Number the rows inside each category, then keep the first N. With row number, you get exactly two per category. Emma and Ulysses tie at three ten, and only one of them makes it. Use rank instead, and both tied books come back: five rows. Question five. What is qualify? It filters on a window function the way having filters on an aggregate, with no wrapping query. DuckDB, Snowflake and BigQuery support it. Now the part people most often get wrong: the frame.",
